main.content:
==English==
{{swp}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=distribute|:inh|enm:casten<id:throw>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|casten}}, from {{der|en|non|kasta||to throw, cast, overturn}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*kastōną||to throw, cast}}, of unknown origin.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|cast||to cast, throw}}, {{cog|da|kaste||to throw}}, {{cog|sv|kasta||to throw, cast, fling, toss, discard}}, {{cog|is|kasta||to pitch, toss}}. In the sense of "flinging", displaced native {{m|en|warp}}.

The senses relating to broadcasting are based on that same term; compare [[-cast]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|käst|a=RP}}, {{IPA|en|/kɑːst/}}
* {{enPR|kăst|a=Northern England,Scotland}}, {{IPA|en|/kast/}}
* {{enPR|kăst|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/kæst/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-cast.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{enPR|a=New York,New Orleans,Philadelphia,Baltimore,æ-tensing|/kɛəst/}}
* {{homophones|en|caste|;|karst<a:in some <<non-rhotic>> accents>}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑːst|æst|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{wikidata lexeme|L735}}
{{en-verb|+|+|~,+<l:nonstandard>}}
[[File:Breakfast for Safi.jpg|thumb|A child with '''cast''' legs after surgery (14).]]

# {{lb|en|physical}} {{non-gloss|To move, or be moved, away.}}
## {{lb|en|now|_|somewhat|_|literary}} To [[throw]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|fling|hurl|Thesaurus:throw}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|text=Why then a Ladder quaintly made of Cords / To '''cast''' vp, with a paire of anchoring hookes, / Would serue to scale another Hero's towre{{nb...}}.}}
##* {{RQ:Sterne Tristram Shandy|page=262|text=The more, an' please your honour, the pity, said the Corporal; in uttering which, he '''cast''' his spade into the wheelbarrow{{nb...}}.}}
## To throw forward (a [[fishing line]], [[net]] etc.) into the sea. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|project}}
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Matthew|4|text=As Jesus walked by the see off Galile, he sawe two brethren: Simon which was called Peter, and Andrew his brother, '''castynge''' a neet into the see (for they were fisshers){{nb...}}.}}
## To [[throw down]] or [[aside]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|cast down|throw down}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=XII|passage=So she to Guyon offred it to tast; / Who taking it out of her tender hond, / The cup to ground did violently '''cast''', / That all in peeces it was broken fond {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|6|30|text=it is profitable for thee that one of thy members should perish, and not that thy whole body should be '''cast''' into hell.}}
##* {{RQ:Time|date=19 Dec 1930|title=Sidar the Madman
|passage=Near Puerto Limon, Costa Rica, Madman, co-pilot and plane were caught in a storm, '''cast''' into the Caribbean, drowned.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=w:Hilary Mantel|title=w:Wolf Hall|page=316|publisher=Fourth Estate|year_published=2010
|passage=Her bow is not to her liking. In a temper, she '''casts''' it on the grass.}}
## {{lb|en|of an animal}} To throw off (the [[skin]]) as a process of growth; to [[shed]] the [[hair]] or [[fur]] of the [[coat]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|slough}}
## {{lb|en|botany}} To shed leaves or fruit prematurely.
## {{lb|en|obsolete|_|except in set phrases}} To [[remove]], [[take off]] (clothes). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|doff}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1822|title=Life of Donald McBane|journal=w:Blackwood's Magazine|volume=12|page=745
|passage=when the serjeant saw me, he '''cast''' his coat and put it on me, and they carried me on their shoulders to a village where the wounded were and our surgeons{{nb...}}.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2 March 2002|author=Jess Cartner-Morley|title=How to Wear Clothes|journal=The Guardian
|passage=You know the saying, "Ne'er '''cast''' a clout till May is out"? Well, personally, I'm bored of my winter clothes by March.}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} To [[heave]] the [[lead]] and [[line]] in order to [[ascertain]] the [[depth]] of [[water]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[vomit]].
##: {{syn|en|hurl|Thesaurus:regurgitate}}
##* {{RQ:Jonson Poetaster|1|21|passage=These verses{{...}}make me ready to '''cast'''.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To throw up, as a mound, or rampart.
##* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|19|48|passage=Thine enemies shall '''cast''' a trench [bank] about thee.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To throw out or emit; to [[exhale]].
##* '''1695''' (first published), '''1726''' (final dated of publication) {{w|John Woodward}}, ''An Essay toward a Natural History of the Earth and Terrestrial Bodies''
##*: This{{...}}'''casts''' a sulphurous smell.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1849|author=w:Philip Henry Gosse|title=[[s:Natural History, Birds|Natural History]]
|passage=This horned bird, as it '''casts''' a strong smell, so it hath a foul look, much exceeding the European Raven in bigness}}
# To [[direct]] (one's eyes, gaze etc.). {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|text=To whom do Lyons '''cast''' their gentle Lookes? Not to the Beast, that would vsurpe their Den.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|I|11|text=She then yawned again, threw aside her book, and '''cast''' her eyes round the room in quest of some amusement{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Heyer Unknown Ajax|chapter=1| passage=But Richmond, his grandfather's darling, after one thoughtful glance '''cast''' under his lashes at that uncompromising countenance appeared to lose himself in his own reflections.}}
# {{lb|en|dated|_|except in|_|accounting}} To [[add up]] (a [[column]] of [[figures]], accounts etc.); cross-cast refers to adding up a row of figures. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|sum|tally|Thesaurus:add up}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Jew of Malta|20|I|passage=To what this ten years' tribute will amount,<br>That we have '''cast''', but cannot compass it<br>By reason of the wars, that robb'd our store}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|text=The Clearke of Chartam: hee can write and / reade, and '''cast''' accompt.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=17|book=II|passage=I cannot yet '''cast''' account either with penne or Counters.}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Robinson Crusoe|text=I '''cast''' up the notches on my post, and found I had been on shore three hundred and sixty-five days.}}
# {{lb|en|social}} {{non-gloss|To predict, to decide, to plan.}}
## {{lb|en|astrology}} To [[calculate]] the astrological value of (a horoscope, birth etc.). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|page=309|edition=2001|passage=he is{{...}}a perfect astrologer, that can '''cast''' the rise and fall of others, and mark their errant motions to his own use.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author={{w|Keith Thomas (historian)|Keith Thomas}}|title=Religion and the Decline of Magic|page=332|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2012
|passage=John Gadbury confessed that Mrs Cellier, ‘the Popish Midwife’, had asked him to '''cast''' the King's nativity, although the astrology claimed to have refused to do so.}}
##* '''1985''', {{w|Lawrence Durrell}}, ''{{w|Quinx}}'', Faber & Faber 2004 {{qualifier|Avignon Quintet}}, p.1197:
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[plan]], [[intend]]. {{defdate|14th|19th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1485|author=w:Thomas Malory|title={{w|Le Morte Darthur}} Book XIX, Chapter i [https://quod.lib.umich.edu/c/cme/MaloryWks2/1:21.1?rgn=div2;view=fulltext leaf 386v]|text=[...] ''for the quene had '''cast''' to haue ben ageyne with kyng Arthur at the ferthest by ten of the clok / and soo was that tyme her purpoos.''<br>[...] "for the queen had '''cast''' to have been again with King Arthur at the furthest by ten of the clock, and so was that time her purpose."}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|II|i|passage=I wrapt my selfe in Palmers weed, / And '''cast''' to seeke him forth through daunger and great dreed.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1685|author={{w|Sir William Temple, 1st Baronet|William Temple}}|title="Upon the Gardens of Epicurus
|passage=The cloister{{...}}had, I doubt not, been '''cast''' for [an orange-house].}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[assign]] (a [[role]] in a [[play]] or [[performance]]). {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The director '''cast''' the part carefully.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[assign]] a [[role]] in a [[play]] or [[performance]] to (an actor).
##: {{ux|en|The director '''cast''' John Smith as King Lear.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[describe]] in an opinionated way. {{n-g|Mostly used with a metaphor involving [[light]].}}
##: {{ux|en|King John '''cast''' his predecessor in a negative light to deflect criticism of his own questionable decisions.}}
## To consider; to turn or revolve in the mind; to plan.
##: {{ux|en|to '''cast''' about for reasons}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|1|29|text=She{{...}}'''cast''' in her mind what manner of salutation this should be.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To impose; to bestow; to rest.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|I|ii|passage=The government I '''cast''' upon my brother.}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|4|22|text='''Cast''' thy burden upon the Lord.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To defeat in a lawsuit; to decide against; to convict.
##: {{ux|en|to be '''cast''' in damages}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1822|author=w:John Galt|title=The Provost
|passage=She was '''cast''' to be hanged.}}
##* {{RQ:Allestree Decay
|passage=Were the case referred to any competent judge, {{...}}they would inevitably be '''cast'''.}}
## {{senseid|en|decide}} To turn (the balance or scale); to overbalance; hence, to make [[preponderate]]; to decide.
##: {{syn|en|decide|determine|Thesaurus:choose}}
##: {{ux|en|a '''casting''' voice}}
##* '''24 July, 1659''', {{w|Robert South}}, ''Interest Deposed, and Truth Restored''
# To perform, bring forth (a magical [[spell]] or enchantment).
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=[[w:PC Gamer|PC Gamer (US)]]|date=February 2025|number=392|page=93|title=Reinstall: ''Path of Exile''|author=Tyler Colp
|text=While I could tell my little witch was growing stronger with each piece of loot, I got tired of '''casting''' fireball after fireball and looting gear with three more health.}}
# To [[throw]] (light etc.) on or upon something, or in a given direction.
#: {{syn|en|pour|shed|Thesaurus:illuminate}}
#* {{RQ:Time|date=24 April 1950|title=A Global View
|passage=The threat of Russian barbarism sweeping over the free world will '''cast''' its ominous shadow over us for many, many years.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1960|author=w:Lawrence Durrell|title={{w|Clea (novel)|Clea}}
|passage=A sudden thought '''cast''' a gloom over his countenance.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1972|lyricist=Ian Anderson|title=w:Thick As A Brick|artist={{w|Jethro Tull (band)|Jethro Tull}}|passage=The Poet and the Painter<br>'''Casting''' shadows on the water<br>As the sun plays on the infantry<br>Returning from the sea.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To give birth to (a child) [[prematurely]]; to [[miscarry]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes}}, Folio Society, 2006, vol.1, p.98:
#*: being with childe, they may without feare of accusation, spoyle and '''cast''' {{transterm|frm|avorter}} their children, with certaine medicaments, which they have only for that purpose.
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|edition=2nd|V|20|text=The abortion of a woman they describe by an horse kicking a wolf; because a mare will '''cast''' her foal if she tread in the track of that animal.}}
# {{senseid|en|metal}} To shape (molten metal etc.) by [[pour]]ing into a [[mould]]; to make (an object) in such a way. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Time|date=24 March 1923|title=Rodin's Death
|passage=One copy of the magnificent caveman, The Thinker, of which Rodin '''cast''' several examples in bronze, is seated now in front of the Detroit Museum of Art, where it was placed last autumn.}}
## {{lb|en|printing|dated}} To [[stereotype]] or [[electrotype]].
# To [[twist]] or [[warp]] (of fabric, timber etc.). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1680|author=w:Joseph Moxon|title=The Art of Joinery
|passage=Stuff is said to '''cast''' or warp when{{...}}it alters its flatness or straightness.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To bring the [[bow]]s of a [[sailing ship]] on to the required [[tack]] just as the [[anchor]] is [[weighed]] by use of the [[headsail]]; to bring (a ship) round. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# To [[deposit]] (a ballot or voting paper); to formally register (one's vote). {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Time to tell all the ballots '''cast''' by voters in the box.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} To [[change]] a [[variable]] [[type]] from, for example, [[integer]] to [[real]], or integer to text. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|To display a number, you need to '''cast''' it to a string type.}}
# {{lb|en|hunting}} Of dogs, hunters: to [[spread out]] and [[search]] for a [[scent]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1955|author=w:William Golding|title={{w|The Inheritors (William Golding)|The Inheritors}}|publisher=Faber and Faber|year_published=2005|page=50
|passage=He clambered on to an apron of rock that held its area out to the sun and began to '''cast''' across it. The direction of the wind changed and the scent touched him again.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} To [[set]] (a bone etc.) in a cast.
#: {{ux|en|They will '''cast''' his arm in a plaster because he broke his fibula.}}
# {{lb|en|Wicca}} To open a [[circle]] in order to begin a [[spell]] or meeting of [[witch]]es.
# {{lb|en|media}} To [[broadcast]] (video) over the [[Internet]] or a local network, especially to one's [[television]].
#: {{ux|en|The streamer was the first to '''cast''' footage of the new game.}}

